WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.124
Files are security boundaries.

2
00:00:02.124 --> 00:00:10.853
Uploaded files, generated reports, temporary files, archives, configuration files, and imported data sets all deserve caution.

3
00:00:10.853 --> 00:00:16.516
A filename supplied by a user or partner system should not be treated as a trusted path.

4
00:00:16.516 --> 00:00:21.943
A content type supplied by a client should not be treated as proof of file contents.

5
00:00:21.943 --> 00:00:29.493
File size, extension, storage location, permissions, cleanup behavior, and downstream processing all matter.

6
00:00:29.493 --> 00:00:36.650
Path handling mistakes often begin with string concatenation and assumptions about where a file will land.

7
00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:48.919
Defensive code uses safe path APIs, normalizes paths carefully, constrains access to intended directories, avoids writing into executable locations, and treats archives with care.

8
00:00:48.919 --> 00:00:57.728
Archive extraction risks at a high level include unexpected paths, large expanded content, and files that affect later processing.

9
00:00:57.728 --> 00:01:03.862
Temporary files also need safe creation, permissions, lifecycle management, and cleanup.

10
00:01:03.862 --> 00:01:05.829
Parsing is another boundary.

11
00:01:05.829 --> 00:01:13.772
JSON is familiar, but parsed JSON is still untrusted data until validated for the expected schema and business meaning.

12
00:01:13.772 --> 00:01:22.109
YAML and XML parsers can have security-sensitive options, depending on how they are configured and what features they allow.

13
00:01:22.109 --> 00:01:34.299
The durable lesson is to choose parsers deliberately, use safe modes where available, disable unnecessary features, enforce size limits, and validate parsed structures before use.

14
00:01:34.299 --> 00:01:38.546
Unsafe deserialization deserves special attention in Python.

15
00:01:38.546 --> 00:01:44.366
Pickle is designed for Python object serialization, not for accepting untrusted data.

16
00:01:44.366 --> 00:01:52.782
Loading serialized objects from untrusted or poorly controlled sources can create dangerous behavior depending on context.

17
00:01:52.782 --> 00:01:57.815
Safer designs prefer data formats with clear schemas and limited behavior.

18
00:01:57.815 --> 00:02:07.804
When file handling, parsing, or deserialization touches user-controlled input, treat it as a high-value review area rather than ordinary plumbing.
